00:14:25,708 --> 00:14:34,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I can't postpone it, if it feels like I can't postpone it, would then even giving in once be against kamasumi cācara?

188
00:14:37,793 --> 00:14:40,054
[SPEAKER_01]: If it is the extent of obsession as measured, then yes.

189
00:14:40,934 --> 00:14:41,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

190
00:14:41,598 --> 00:14:41,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it would.

191
00:14:41,978 --> 00:14:42,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

192
00:14:42,459 --> 00:14:42,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

193
00:14:42,839 --> 00:14:44,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no quantity.

194
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:56,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if there were quantity, basically written within Kāma-sumicācara, it wouldn't serve the practical purpose of training.

195
00:14:58,068 --> 00:15:00,310
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why even being celibate,

196
00:15:01,274 --> 00:15:03,336
[SPEAKER_00]: It can still be on the level of sīlabhāta paramāsa.

197
00:15:04,156 --> 00:15:05,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, you're not training in it.

198
00:15:06,458 --> 00:15:07,819
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not taking on those values.

199
00:15:07,839 --> 00:15:10,902
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just not doing this, and then abstaining from that.

200
00:15:11,622 --> 00:15:27,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you give in, or within the precepts of kamasumi cācara, you could recognize, okay, somebody might actually give in multiple... do certain things multiple times.

201
00:15:28,762 --> 00:15:31,104
[SPEAKER_00]: And still not necessarily be against kamasamhicacara.

202
00:15:33,005 --> 00:15:44,854
[SPEAKER_00]: But on different occasions, the same thing, depending on the level of obsession, the animality of the mind, the obsession behind it, it's like even giving once would be misconduct in sensuality.

203
00:15:45,935 --> 00:15:46,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's...

204
00:15:46,555 --> 00:15:47,055
[SPEAKER_00]: There's this point.

205
00:15:47,075 --> 00:15:49,858
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't quantify it by any external measure.

206
00:15:50,923 --> 00:15:57,886
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can quantify it by recognizing the extent of the pressure, the extent of the urgency.

207
00:15:59,927 --> 00:16:02,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So you said, can you postpone it?

208
00:16:03,028 --> 00:16:11,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Are there any other practical considerations, questions, reflections they can do, again, to clarify where the line is?

209
00:16:12,253 --> 00:16:17,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you do something else to appease that pressure, rather than that specific thing?

210
00:16:18,622 --> 00:16:26,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would an alternative to that which you want satisfy the desire?

211
00:16:29,404 --> 00:16:30,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Less harmful, you could say.

212
00:16:31,045 --> 00:16:32,445
[SPEAKER_01]: A less harmful alternative, right.

213
00:16:34,145 --> 00:16:34,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything else?

214
00:16:39,026 --> 00:16:47,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there any, even mundane, negative consequence that would come from you doing that action, that you would have to ignore?

215
00:16:48,713 --> 00:16:49,354
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to do it.

216
00:16:50,155 --> 00:16:52,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that sounds like the first thing you should probably think about.

217
00:16:52,217 --> 00:16:52,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

218
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:56,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Potentially upsetting other people.

219
00:16:56,943 --> 00:16:57,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

220
00:16:57,884 --> 00:16:58,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting blamed.

221
00:16:59,045 --> 00:17:00,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting blamed.

222
00:17:03,070 --> 00:17:04,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I guess you could say, you know...

223
00:17:06,752 --> 00:17:06,992
[SPEAKER_00]: What?

224
00:17:08,033 --> 00:17:10,075
[SPEAKER_00]: The degree of embarrassment you would feel.

225
00:17:10,495 --> 00:17:12,036
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody saw you doing that.

226
00:17:12,136 --> 00:17:12,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

227
00:17:12,917 --> 00:17:13,157
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

228
00:17:14,318 --> 00:17:15,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's an animal.

229
00:17:15,198 --> 00:17:17,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, an animal doesn't care who's watching.

230
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:17,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

231
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:32,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, when one is gradually... inevitably having to gradually work towards the eight precepts, the... you would be... you would have to be giving up to... giving in, sorry, to the animal's pressure.

232
00:17:33,591 --> 00:17:37,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, even that does not justify loss of perspective.

233
00:17:38,719 --> 00:17:42,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Or more loss than you already... Yeah, of course.

234
00:17:43,947 --> 00:17:45,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthering of the loss of perspective.

235
00:17:47,011 --> 00:17:47,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

236
00:17:50,303 --> 00:17:59,249
[SPEAKER_00]: You could then even ask yourself, say you have a desire to eat something very specific, because also, factually, you are hungry.

237
00:18:00,050 --> 00:18:02,131
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the animal mind is factually hungry.

238
00:18:02,251 --> 00:18:03,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the body is hungry.

239
00:18:03,432 --> 00:18:05,814
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal wants to eat.

240
00:18:06,014 --> 00:18:08,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Citta is pressuring you towards food.

241
00:18:09,116 --> 00:18:13,519
[SPEAKER_00]: You would know for a fact that if you were to eat anything, it would actually satisfy the hunger.

242
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:17,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's agreeable stuff, you don't have to eat stuff that you hate.

243
00:18:20,786 --> 00:18:25,831
[SPEAKER_00]: And not eat that specific, like, extra specific things that you crave to eat.

244
00:18:27,112 --> 00:18:39,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on one way, you would provide a satisfaction to the pressure and the animal, even out of a degree of weakness, but on another way, you wouldn't allow it that cherry on top that really want it.

245
00:18:40,544 --> 00:18:42,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And that principle can actually be applied in regard to everything.

246
00:18:43,915 --> 00:18:56,105
[SPEAKER_00]: You wanna... again, you're not able to keep the eight precepts, it's too much pressure, it's too painful, taking on five, but not blindly, like a just sort of mindless, self-declared religious Buddhist, but actually the one that wants to train.

247
00:18:57,346 --> 00:18:59,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Music, uh... entertainment.

248
00:19:00,388 --> 00:19:11,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, there's specific things you want, out of boredom, out of anxiety, out of all these unwholesome bases that, of course, you have to deal with, if you actually want to practice for Sotāpati, at some point, but...

249
00:19:12,474 --> 00:19:15,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you have to give in, to some extent.

250
00:19:15,115 --> 00:19:16,516
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just too much, as we said before.

251
00:19:17,797 --> 00:19:19,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Even then, you could start applying the same principle.

252
00:19:20,438 --> 00:19:22,239
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need a distraction.

253
00:19:22,299 --> 00:19:29,102
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is just getting too... too unpleasant, too forceful, too strong.

254
00:19:30,542 --> 00:19:34,784
[SPEAKER_00]: To what extent you allow that... that desire to be satisfied?

255
00:19:37,254 --> 00:19:37,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

256
00:19:38,254 --> 00:19:42,897
[SPEAKER_01]: From the average person's point of view, that would already be a training, because... That would be a training, exactly, yeah.

257
00:19:43,677 --> 00:19:48,919
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the... that's the difference between keeping the precepts and training in them.

258
00:19:49,099 --> 00:19:51,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention is taking them as a standard.

259
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:51,460
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

260
00:19:54,042 --> 00:19:54,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

261
00:19:55,862 --> 00:20:01,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Inevitably, you will feel, when you give in, you will feel relieved,

262
00:20:02,715 --> 00:20:10,302
[SPEAKER_00]: To some extent, because again, you are a craving, acting out of compulsion that gets appeased.

263
00:20:11,563 --> 00:20:13,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would feel relieved, you would feel satisfied.

264
00:20:14,846 --> 00:20:24,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you pay attention to the extent of your engagement with the sense objects, try and change, try and kind of relegate the sense object down from the one that you want,

265
00:20:25,597 --> 00:20:30,547
[SPEAKER_00]: And force your mind to not quite get what it wanted through and through, basically.

266
00:20:31,409 --> 00:20:33,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And build up on that.

